han joint-ILP on PRIMES (157 s vs 277 s) and dominates joint-ILP entirely for the random-target M=3 case. (iv) Catalog-size empirical fact: `#Θ(6, W)` = (1458, 30,898, 218,066) at W=(1, 2, 3). **Refines E5.3** with: PRIMES `M*(W=2; N=6) = 4` is *not* breakable by replacing PRIMES with density-matched random — the random class is at least as hard at W=2. **Refines C7-S89/E1.6** (PRIMES ≈ oddness) by showing the W=2 PRIMES-vs-random gap holds in direction even *outside* the calibrated-oddness regime where bit_0 mass is matched. Time-asymmetry observation parallel to S127: PRIMES W=2 M=4 SAT in 181 s (column-enum) / 17 s (S127 joint), random W=2 M=4 UNKNOWN at 618 s — PRIMES carries low-rank exploitable structure (most plausibly the strong bit-0 predictor) that random of identical density lacks. **Cross-domain ingredient**: NONE (internal cross-encoding, not cross-domain). **Successors proposed (S135)**: C8.b.i greedy/LNS SAT search on random M=4 W=2 (1 session); C8.b.ii W=3 column-enum on random K=218K (1-2 sessions); C8.b.iii seed-distribution histogram at N=6 W=2 M=3 across 100+ seeds in 32-core parallel ≈ 10 min wall-clock (1 session). Cites E5.3, E1.6, S84, S127 column-enumeration framework. See `experiments/constructions/d2_sign_threshold_w_m_tradeoff/random_n6_resolution/`, `archive/sessions/session135_c8b_random_n6_resolution.md`. | 135 |
